  What SpotBugs and find-sec-bugs are told not to report, and why each one is not
  a hole (REQ-SEC-076).

  Every entry here is a pattern that is WRONG for this codebase rather than one
  that is inconvenient. A suppression added to make a build green is how a
  security tool becomes a formality, so each carries the reason it does not
  apply — and a reader who disagrees has something specific to argue with.

  Nothing that describes a real property of this code is in here. The three
  findings that did were fixed rather than excluded: a locale-dependent
  comparison deciding a column name, a case fold on upload bytes immediately
  before a security decision, and a NullPointerException used as a conditional.
-->
<FindBugsFilter>

  <!--
    CRLF_INJECTION_LOGS fires on every parameterised log statement carrying a
    value that came from outside. It is worth taking seriously and it is already
    answered twice over here:

      * TextInputModule strips every control character from every string that
        arrives through JSON, at the edge, before anything sees it (REQ-SEC-032).
        A newline cannot reach a log line through the API;
      * the deployment logs JSON lines (REQ-NFR-041), where the encoder escapes a
        newline inside a value rather than ending the record.

    A forged log line needs both of those to fail. What this rule would buy in
    exchange is a suppression comment on every log statement in the codebase.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="CRLF_INJECTION_LOGS"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SE_TRANSIENT_FIELD_NOT_RESTORED on our exception types. They carry an id or a
    field name and are never serialised: they exist to cross a method boundary
    and be turned into a problem document. `Exception` is `Serializable` by
    inheritance, which is the only reason the rule looks at them at all.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SE_TRANSIENT_FIELD_NOT_RESTORED,SE_BAD_FIELD"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*Exception"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW. Several configuration holders validate in their
    constructor and refuse to be built — UrlSigningKey, TrustedProxies,
    MediaHostCheck, MediaUrlSigner, PasswordHashingProperties. That is the
    design: a missing or wrong security-relevant setting aborts startup rather
    than producing an object that works and is wrong (CLAUDE.md, Security;
    REQ-NFR-046). The partially-constructed-object attack the rule guards against
    needs a subclass, and every one of these types is final or a Spring
    singleton nobody extends.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="CT_CONSTRUCTOR_THROW"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Lombok generates the constructors and getters, and SpotBugs reports on the
    bytecode: a @RequiredArgsConstructor storing a collection parameter is
    EI_EXPOSE_REP2 every time. Copying every argument by hand is the boilerplate
    Lombok removes, on Spring beans holding other Spring beans.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Generated protobuf and gRPC classes. Not ours to fix, and SpotBugs has a
    great deal to say about generated builders.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Package name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\.plugin\.v1.*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    Spring injects into @Value fields and SpotBugs cannot see it, so each reads
    as never written and then as possibly null.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="UWF_UNWRITTEN_FIELD,NP_UNWRITTEN_FIELD,UWF_FIELD_NOT_INITIALIZED_IN_CONSTRUCTOR,NP_UNWRITTEN_PUBLIC_OR_PROTECTED_FIELD"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    RCN_REDUNDANT_NULLCHECK_OF_NONNULL_VALUE inside try-with-resources. The
    compiler generates the null check; the source has none.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="RCN_REDUNDANT_NULLCHECK_OF_NONNULL_VALUE"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\..*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SPRING_ENDPOINT is informational: it reports that a method is a Spring
    endpoint, which every method in `rest` is by design. It exists to give a
    reviewer an inventory, and an inventory that fails a build is a build that
    fails for having endpoints.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SPRING_ENDPOINT"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    WEAK_TRUST_MANAGER on the pinned trust manager. The rule assumes a custom trust
    manager is a weakened one; this one is STRICTER than CA validation. The
    deployment's CA signs `api`, `worker`, `blobstore`, `opensearch` and every
    plugin, so accepting anything it signed would let a compromised plugin answer
    as any of them. Pinning the single certificate that may answer is the
    mechanism REQ-SEC-056 asks for, and ADR-0044 is the reason.

    Three classes, one decision: `PinnedCertificate` in `platform` IS the trust
    manager — it moved there on 2026-09-14 when OpenSearch needed the same
    mechanism the blob store already had, and a second copy of a trust decision
    is how two clients come to disagree about what they trust. The other two are
    the clients that install it, and each names its own fingerprint variable in
    the refusal so an operator knows which pin to look at.

    WEAK_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER comes with it: pinning has already answered a stricter
    question than a hostname does, and the certificate is issued to a service name
    a client may legitimately reach under another.

    `RegisteredPlugins` is the same decision pointing the other way, and the only
    one that does: it is the SERVER's view of a client on the host channel
    (ADR-0071), where the core answers and a plugin calls. It accepts a certificate
    whose SHA-256 is the fingerprint SOME registration pins and refuses every other
    one — including one the deployment's own CA signed, which is the case the rule
    cannot distinguish from an empty check. `getAcceptedIssuers` returns nothing
    because the chain is not what decides here, and `checkServerTrusted` throws
    unconditionally, because a manager that recognises callers must never be used
    to decide what to trust as a server.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="WEAK_TRUST_MANAGER,WEAK_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\.(media\.infrastructure\.BlobStoreChannelFactory|platform\.PinnedCertificate|search\.infrastructure\.OpenSearchClientFactory|plugins\.infrastructure\.RegisteredPlugins).*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    UNENCRYPTED_SOCKET on the clamd INSTREAM connection. The clamd protocol has no
    TLS to speak: it is a plain socket by design, on the `scanner` segment, which
    carries this connection and nothing else (ADR-0024, ADR-0037). What travels on
    it is an upload that has not been accepted yet, to a process whose only reply
    is a verdict.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="UNENCRYPTED_SOCKET"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.media.infrastructure.ClamAvScanner"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    COMMAND_INJECTION on the libvips subprocess. There is no shell: ProcessBuilder
    takes a LIST, so every element is one argument and nothing is parsed. The
    elements are the configured binary name, two paths this process created with
    `Files.createTempFile`, and a format string built from an int. No caller-supplied
    value reaches any of them — the image the subprocess reads is a temporary file,
    named by us, whose CONTENT is the untrusted part, which is precisely why it is
    decoded in a subprocess rather than in this one (O29).
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="COMMAND_INJECTION"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.media.infrastructure.VipsImageProcessor"/>
  </Match>

<!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on `IdempotencyKeys`. Reading `Idempotency-Key` is what the class
    is for, and the value is treated as untrusted before anything is done with it:
    trimmed, bounded at 255 characters, and refused unless every character is
    printable ASCII without spaces. What survives is stored as data in a parameterised
    statement and compared; it reaches no response — the conflict deliberately does
    not name the key — and no log line (REQ-API-005).
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.IdempotencyKeys"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on `EntityTags`. Reading `If-Match` is what the class is for, and
    the value is treated as untrusted from the first line: it is trimmed, required to
    be a quoted number, parsed with `Long.parseLong`, and otherwise refused. What
    survives is a `long`. It reaches no response — the refusal deliberately does not
    repeat what arrived — no log line, no query and no header, and the comparison it
    feeds is a numeric one against a column the caller cannot influence
    (REQ-API-004).
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.EntityTags"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on the forwarded-header filter. Reading X-Forwarded-For is what
    the class is for, and it is the one place in the application that does: it
    applies the header only when the PEER is in HOMEINV_TRUSTED_PROXIES, which is
    the check the rule is asking for (REQ-SEC-063).
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.TrustedForwardedHeaderFilter"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    `throws Exception` on the security filter chain bean. It is Spring's own
    signature for HttpSecurity.build(), and narrowing it would mean catching and
    rethrowing an exception whose only correct handling is to fail startup.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="THROWS_METHOD_THROWS_CLAUSE_BASIC_EXCEPTION"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.WebSecurityConfiguration"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    The Hibernate UserType contract hands back the Serializable that `disassemble`
    produced, which for this type is always a String. The cast cannot fail without
    Hibernate handing back somebody else's value.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="BC_UNCONFIRMED_CAST"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.platform.LtreeType"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    And the same for the jsonb type beside it, for the same reason and in the same
    method: `assemble` receives what `disassemble` returned, which is the String
    this type maps.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="BC_UNCONFIRMED_CAST"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.platform.JsonbType"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    PZLA_PREFER_ZERO_LENGTH_ARRAYS where null means ABSENT rather than empty:
    `TrustedProxies.parse` returns null for "not an address" and
    `UrlSigningKey.tryDecodeBase64` for "not base64". A zero-length array would be
    a valid empty value, and both callers branch on exactly that difference.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="PZLA_PREFER_ZERO_LENGTH_ARRAYS"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\.platform\.(TrustedProxies|UrlSigningKey)"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    And the same in `CredentialKey.tryDecodeBase64`, which is the same method for
    the same reason: a key file holding raw bytes is not base64, and null is how
    that is said. An empty array would be a key of length zero, which the length
    check would then reject with the wrong message.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="PZLA_PREFER_ZERO_LENGTH_ARRAYS"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.identity.infrastructure.CredentialKey"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    IMPROPER_UNICODE on TextInputModule's NFC normalisation. Normalising IS the
    class's purpose, and the order is the one the rule wants: normalise, strip
    control characters, trim — and only then does Bean Validation run on the
    result (REQ-SEC-032). Validating before normalising is the mistake; this is
    the other way round.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="IMPROPER_UNICODE"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\.platform\.TextInputModule.*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    IMPROPER_UNICODE on `SearchEngineProperties`. The one case fold left in the
    search configuration reads the URL's scheme, and it is written so that a fold
    can only make the connection STRICTER: everything that is not plainly `http`
    is treated as TLS and therefore as pinned, so a scheme this does not recognise
    becomes a connection that refuses to be made rather than a plaintext one that
    is quietly made.

    The three other findings this class of rule raised on 2026-09-14 were fixed
    rather than excluded, which is why only one entry is here: the engine name
    became a constant on the enum instead of `name().toLowerCase()`, the language
    is folded once where the request is read and compared exactly by every engine,
    and the TLS decision is taken here once instead of in each client.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="IMPROPER_UNICODE"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.search.application.SearchEngineProperties"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    IMPROPER_UNICODE on MediaHostCheck's hostname normalisation. `IDN.toASCII`
    then lowercase is what normalising a hostname IS, and the order is the
    load-bearing part: after the punycode conversion everything is ASCII and the
    case change is exact, so the fold the rule warns about cannot happen.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="IMPROPER_UNICODE"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.platform.MediaHostCheck"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED_NO_SIDE_EFFECT on the two places that rotate a session.
    Both calls are made FOR their effect on the container's session store, and both
    have one: `changeSessionId()` replaces the id an attacker may have planted,
    which is the servlet container's own fixation defence, and `getSession(true)`
    creates the session the security context — or the pending login of REQ-AUTH-002
    — is about to be written into. The rule reads the `HttpServletRequest`
    interface, where neither is declared to mutate anything.

    It named `AuthController.login` until the second factor arrived and the login
    became two calls: the rotation now happens in `PendingLogin.remember`, when the
    password is accepted, and again in `SessionEstablisher.establish`, when the
    session is actually created.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED_NO_SIDE_EFFECT"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.PendingLogin"/>
    <Method name="remember"/>
  </Match>
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="RV_RETURN_VALUE_IGNORED_NO_SIDE_EFFECT"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.SessionEstablisher"/>
    <Method name="establish"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_CONTENT_TYPE on `JsonBodyLimitFilter`. The header is untrusted and is
    treated as such: it is lowercased and compared against one literal prefix to
    decide whether this request carries an upload, and it reaches nothing else —
    not a response, not a log line, not a parser. A caller who lies about it gets
    the stricter limit, which is the safe direction.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_CONTENT_TYPE"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.JsonBodyLimitFilter"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER_USER_AGENT on `SessionEstablisher`. The header is untrusted and is
    treated as one: control characters are stripped and the length is bounded before
    it is stored, it authorises nothing, nothing is looked up with it, and it is read
    back only by the account it belongs to — the session overview of REQ-AUTH-009,
    where it is what tells "my phone" from "the machine at work". A parsed one would
    be a guess presented as a fact, which is why it is stored as it arrived.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER_USER_AGENT"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.SessionEstablisher"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on the two places that read `Authorization`: the filter that
    authenticates a service account (REQ-AUTH-010) and the CSRF matcher that
    exempts a request carrying one. The header IS the credential here, which is the
    one case where reading it is the whole point — it is compared against a stored
    SHA-256 and reaches nothing else: not a response, not a log line, not a query
    built from it. A caller who lies about it is refused with `unauthenticated`,
    which is the safe direction.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="~de\.greluc\.homeinv\.rest\.(ServiceAccountAuthenticationFilter|WebSecurityConfiguration).*"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on `TraceIdFilter`. `traceparent` is untrusted, which is why
    the trace id is matched against `[0-9a-f]{32}` and rejected when it is the
    all-zero id, before anything is done with it. Unvalidated it would end up in
    a log line, which is the injection the rule is about; validated it is 32 hex
    characters. It authorises nothing and nothing is looked up with it.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.TraceIdFilter"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SPRING_CSRF_UNRESTRICTED_REQUEST_MAPPING on `ProblemErrorController`. The
    mapping names no method on purpose: the container forwards to `/error` after a
    request has already been refused, whatever method that request used, and one
    restricted to GET would answer a failed POST with a second failure. It reads
    two servlet attributes and writes a problem document; it changes no state, so
    there is nothing for a forged request to achieve.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SPRING_CSRF_UNRESTRICTED_REQUEST_MAPPING"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.ProblemErrorController"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER_USER_AGENT on `AuditTrailInterceptor`. The header is read to
    record WHAT MADE a change, which REQ-SEC-068 asks for by name, and it is
    untrusted exactly as the rule says.

    So it is treated as untrusted rather than excused. Control characters are
    replaced before the value goes anywhere, because a newline in a header is how
    a caller forges what looks like a second log line; and it is cut to the 300
    characters the column takes, because a longer one would be refused by the
    database after the change had already committed — and the failure is
    swallowed on purpose there, so the entry would simply not exist.

    It reaches one bound parameter in one INSERT and is rendered nowhere. There
    is no path from it to a page, a command or a query.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER_USER_AGENT"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.AuditTrailInterceptor"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_PARAMETER on `ItemController.listItems`. It is the one place in the
    application that reads a query parameter through the servlet API rather than
    through `@RequestParam`, and it has to be: Spring converts a single value into
    a `List<String>` by splitting it on commas, and the comma is the `in`
    separator of this API's own filter grammar (08 8.2). One
    `filter=attr.tag:in:a,b` would arrive as two filters while two `filter=`
    parameters would not.

    The value is treated as untrusted, which is what the rule is really asking:
    it is bounded in count and length, then parsed by a grammar that refuses
    anything not of the shape `attr.<key>:<op>:<value>`, and the field it names is
    checked against `TypeRegistry.queryableFields` — the tenant's own allowlist —
    before any statement sees it. Every value ends up as a bound parameter, never
    as SQL (REQ-SEC-031). What a caller typed decides which rows come back and
    nothing else.

    Every other endpoint reads untrusted parameters too; the rule cannot see them
    because Spring binds those.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_PARAMETER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.ItemController"/>
    <Method name="listItems"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    VA_FORMAT_STRING_USES_NEWLINE on the three statements whose text search
    configuration is chosen at runtime. The rule wants `%n`
    instead of a literal newline in a format string, and here the format string is
    a SQL text block whose newlines are the statement's own layout. `%n` would
    emit the platform separator into SQL, which PostgreSQL neither needs nor
    notices.

    More to the point, the alternative the rule pushes towards is forbidden here:
    building the statement by concatenation trips
    `ArchitectureRulesTest.sqlIsNeverConcatenated`, which enforces REQ-SEC-031 —
    a SQL literal joined by `+` to an expression is the one shape a value can
    enter a statement through. The two gates disagree and the project's own rule
    wins; the text block keeps every variable part to `%s` placeholders whose
    arguments come from closed sets.

    `TagAdapter` and `LocationTreeQueries` joined it on 2026-09-14 with the other
    half of REQ-SRCH-011: a tag and a location name are matched as full text in
    the caller's language, so the configuration goes into the statement while the
    text itself stays a parameter. Two of them, `german` and `english`, chosen by
    an `equals` on a value that arrived from a closed set - and it cannot be a
    parameter for the same reason it cannot be one in `ItemSearchAdapter`:
    `to_tsvector` is immutable only with a literal configuration, which is what
    07 7.3 says beside the generated columns.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="VA_FORMAT_STRING_USES_NEWLINE"/>
    <Class name="~de[.]greluc[.]homeinv[.](inventory[.]infrastructure[.]ItemSearchAdapter|tagging[.]infrastructure[.]TagAdapter|locations[.]infrastructure[.]LocationTreeQueries)"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    WEAK_MESSAGE_DIGEST_SHA1 on the password policy's breach lookup. SHA-1 is
    here by PROTOCOL rather than by choice: the k-anonymity range query every
    such service answers is defined over it (ADR-0067), and a different digest
    would mean a port no existing service can serve.

    It is a lookup key and not a credential store. The password itself is hashed
    with Argon2id (REQ-SEC-010) and this digest is never stored, never compared
    against a stored value and never used to authenticate anything. Five of its
    forty characters leave the deployment; the comparison happens in the core, so
    a plugin learns neither the password nor its full hash.
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="WEAK_MESSAGE_DIGEST_SHA1"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.identity.application.DefaultPasswordPolicy"/>
  </Match>

  <!--
    SERVLET_HEADER on the API usage interceptor. The rule is right that a header
    is untrusted input; the whole design of that tag is the answer to it.

    `X-Home-Inv-Client` is read, matched against a regular expression bounded at
    32 characters, lower-cased and then checked for membership in a CLOSED SET of
    six product names. Anything that fails any of those three is `other`. The
    value never reaches a query, a log line, a response or a file path - it
    becomes one of seven constants and nothing else, which is exactly the
    validation the rule asks for and more than it can see.

    The reason it is validated so hard is not injection but CARDINALITY: a
    Prometheus series exists per combination of tag values, so a tag a caller can
    choose freely is an outage waiting for the right request (REQ-API-009,
    `ApiUsageMetricIT`).
  -->
  <Match>
    <Bug pattern="SERVLET_HEADER"/>
    <Class name="de.greluc.homeinv.rest.ApiUsageInterceptor"/>
  </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
